
A temporary shelf for your files on Windows. Collect files from different folders, then drag them all out together into another folder, an email, a browser upload, or any app that accepts dropped files.

FileDock only remembers where your files are. It never copies, moves, renames, or uploads them.

How it works

1. A thin handle sits on the edge of your screen.
2. Drag files from File Explorer toward it. The shelf opens instantly.
3. Drop them in. Repeat from as many folders as you like.
4. Drag the "Drag all N items" strip (or a selection) into your destination.

The shelf keeps your items until you remove them, so you can reuse them as often as you need.

Features

- Edge handle that stays out of the way. It is about 10 px wide, expands on hover or when you drag files over it, and auto-collapses. It can also be pinned open.
- Native drag and drop in both directions. Outgoing drags are copy-only, so your originals are never moved or deleted.
- Files and folders, with duplicates ignored and missing files clearly marked.
- Undo for removing items or clearing the shelf.
- Remembers your shelf between restarts. Everything is stored locally, with no network access, telemetry, or accounts.
- System tray icon, plus a global show/hide shortcut (default Ctrl+Alt+Space, changeable in Settings).
- Left or right edge placement, multi-monitor support, and a start-with-Windows option (off by default).
- Follows your Windows light/dark theme and accent colour.

Download

Grab FileDock.exe from the Releases (../../releases) page. It is a single self-contained file, so no .NET installation is needed.

Requirements: Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit.

▎ The executable is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may warn on first run. Choose More info → Run anyway.

To exit completely, right-click the tray icon and choose Exit.

Build from source

Requires the .NET 10 SDK (https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet/10.0).

dotnet build FileDock.slnx
dotnet test FileDock.slnx
dotnet publish src\FileDock\FileDock.csproj -p:PublishProfile=win-x64

The published file appears at publish\win-x64\FileDock.exe.

Tech

C# · .NET 10 · WPF (MVVM) · Win32/Shell interop · System.Text.Json · xUnit

Known limitations

- Only real files and folders can be added. Items that exist only inside another app, such as files inside a .zip or Outlook attachments, can't be staged.
- Dropping into apps running as administrator won't work unless FileDock runs elevated too (a Windows restriction).
- Tested on Windows 10 with a single display. Windows 11, multi-monitor setups, Outlook, and screen readers are untested.

Data location

%LOCALAPPDATA%\FileDock\ holds shelf.json, settings.json, and a small capped log. Delete the folder to reset the app.

